// hoist-guard — SSC3-J4a's guard, and the only program that exercises it.
//
// `k` is assigned a literal at the TOP LEVEL of the loop body and reassigned, also at top level,
// before the next iteration — so the register holding it is written more than once and the first
// write is exactly the shape `Optimize.hoist` looks for. Lifting it would leave 99 standing for
// every iteration after the first: 10 + 10 + 10 = 30 would become 10 + 99 + 99 = 208. The
// `writes(d) == 1` condition is what refuses the lift.
//
// Measured 2026-08-14: of 127 programs across bench/corpus, v3/tests/front and v3/tests/jit, only
// two had a loop-level `Const` on a multiply-written register and BOTH were corpus files, which no
// gate runs for output — so weakening the guard to `writes(d) >= 1` left every gate GREEN. This
// fixture is that hole closed, and it was itself checked by planting the weakened guard.
def workload(): Int =
  var acc = 0
  var k = 0
  var i = 0
  while i < 3 do
    k = 10
    acc = acc + k
    k = 99
    i = i + 1
  acc

println(workload())
